WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.080
Every smell starts as a map.

2
00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.540
In the mouse olfactory bulb, receptor neurons of one type converge on one

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.520
glomerulus, so an odour lights up a fixed pattern of glomeruli.

4
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:17.700
Piriform cortex does not keep that map.

5
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:21.520
An odour switches on neurons scattered across the cortex.

6
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:26.660
Yet the scattered pattern still knows which odours are chemically alike, and it can

7
00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:27.880
learn what an odour means.

8
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.740
That is the change from bulb to cortex.

9
00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:35.290
Stettler and Axel imaged piriform cortex in living mice.

10
00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:40.690
One odour activated neurons spread across the area, not gathered in a patch.

11
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:45.450
Sosulski and colleagues then traced the output of a single glomerulus.

12
00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.350
In piriform cortex the axons spread diffusely.

13
00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:53.570
In the cortical amygdala, the same glomerulus made tidy patches.

14
00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:58.330
So the scattering belongs to piriform cortex, not to the whole bulb.

15
00:00:59.333 --> 00:01:01.153
Is chemistry lost in the scatter?

16
00:01:02.213 --> 00:01:05.813
Pashkovski and colleagues compared bulb inputs with piriform neurons.

17
00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:11.753
In both, related odours gave related population patterns, and the cortex grouped

18
00:01:11.753 --> 00:01:14.493
related odours more tightly than its input did.

19
00:01:15.273 --> 00:01:17.533
This is a map of similarity, not of place.

20
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:21.553
Similar odours give similar patterns, wherever the cells sit.

21
00:01:22.533 --> 00:01:27.113
Piriform neurons are wired to each other, and those connections are thought to store

22
00:01:27.113 --> 00:01:31.553
experience. Choi and colleagues made artificial ensembles with light.

23
00:01:32.513 --> 00:01:34.893
Paired with a reward, the mice learned one behaviour.

24
00:01:35.893 --> 00:01:37.693
Paired with a shock, they learned the opposite.

25
00:01:38.653 --> 00:01:40.933
So scattered activity can gain learned meaning.

26
00:01:41.673 --> 00:01:44.433
That is not the same as making the mice smell something.

27
00:01:45.467 --> 00:01:47.607
The bulb shapes what the cortex receives.

28
00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:53.167
Granule cells and other interneurons trade signals with mitral and tufted cells

29
00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:57.587
through reciprocal synapses, giving recurrent and lateral inhibition.

30
00:01:58.687 --> 00:02:03.107
Timing matters too, though timing differences do not show which synapses made them.

31
00:02:04.147 --> 00:02:08.927
Sister cells that share one glomerulus had correlated average rates in anaesthetized

32
00:02:08.927 --> 00:02:11.827
mice, but fired at different moments in the breath.

33
00:02:11.827 --> 00:02:14.567
A shared address is not a shared message.

34
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:20.400
So the bulb hands over a map, and the cortex turns it into a scattered pattern that

35
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.480
keeps chemical likeness and learns meaning.

36
00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.800
In mice, that pattern also drifts over weeks.

37
00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:30.220
What stays stable on top of a moving code is still an open question.
